  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough Thursday...\n\nA weak shortwave sliding into the Dakotas has produced isolated \nto scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms over far eastern \nMT this morning. As of this writing the convection is continuing\nto shift east. Another wave is expected to transit the area later\ntoday, spreading isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms \nfrom west to east through the evening, with convection lingering \ninto the early morning hours near the Dakotas border. With modest \ninstability (CAPE values of 500 to 1500 J/kg) and shear (deep \nlayer shear of 20 to 35 kts) in place, a few strong to severe\nthunderstorms are possible this afternoon and evening for areas\nmainly east of Billings. Latest hi-res guidance shows storms more\nisolated in nature this afternoon, forming into more of a line \nthis evening over the east, pushing east into the Dakotas by about\nmidnight. SPC has a marginal risk for severe thunderstorms from \nBillings and areas east. The main threats with thunderstorms are \nlarge hail, strong wind gusts and brief heavy rain. Elsewhere \nacross the area, small hail, gusty winds and brief heavy rain are \nstill possible with thunderstorms.\n\nIndependence Day is forecast to be hot and mostly dry, with highs\nin the 80s to lower 90s. There is a low chance (15%) for showers\nand thunderstorms along the Dakotas border in the late morning and\nafternoon, with any convection diminishing by the evening. Sunday\nlooks to be the hottest day of the next several as ridging builds\nover eastern MT. High temperatures are forecast to range from the\nmiddle 80s to the upper 90s, with some of the hotter valley\nlocations potentially nearing 100 degrees. By the late afternoon\nand evening, a weak shortwave lifting into the region from the\nsouthwest will bring low to moderate chances (20-40%) for showers\nand thunderstorms, spreading east into the overnight. The risk of\na severe storm is low.\n\nMonday through Thursday, hot conditions will continue with highs \ngenerally in the 80s and 90s. Monday looks to be the \"coolest\" day\nbehind a backdoor cold front. Along with the heat, a daily chance\nof showers and thunderstorms continues through the week as \nmoisture (PWATs approaching or exceeding an inch) moving in under\nthe upper level ridge combines with a few weak shortwaves \nrounding the ridge. While the threat for severe thunderstorms is\ncurrently low, will need to keep an eye on the potential. Also,\nwarmer temperatures aloft could create a mid-level cap at times,\nworking to inhibit convection. STP",
